1.Lifecycle Extensions:

-Similar to lifecycle annotations "@BeforeAll, @BeforeEach, @AfterAll, @AfterEach".
-Lifecycle Extensions are used to execute custom logic before/after test classes or methods.






one question might comes to your mind: why we need lifecycle extensions, when we already have annotations like @BeforeAll, @BeforeEach, @AfterAll and @AfterEach?



These lifecycle methods with annotations @BeforeAll, @BeforeEach, @AfterAll and @AfterEach are tied to a single classes
while
Lifecycle extensions are reusable across many test classes




Our custom class implemented an BeforeAllCallBack extension interface:
----------------------------------------------------------------------


public class BeforeAllLifeCycleExtension implements BeforeAllCallback {

	@Override
	public void beforeAll(ExtensionContext context){
		System.out.println("Extension: Before all tests");
	}
	
}


Our custom class implemented an AfterAllCallBack extension interface:
---------------------------------------------------------------------

public class AfterAllLifecycleExtension implements AfterAllCallback{

	@Override
	public void afterAll(ExtensionContext context) throws Exception{
		System.out.println("Extension: After all tests");
	}
}



Execution Context object has all the info about:

-Test class->via getTestClass()

-Test method->via getTestMethod()

-Annotations->accessible through getElement()

-Test instance->via getTestInstance()





